 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Outraged by Kristina's ultimatum, Nastya tells Max to stand his ground because a person is not an animal to be forced into anything, insisting she and Kostya can find new jobs. Max calms them down and says he will go talk to Kristina himself.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Откуда приехал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Из Воронежа.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Как зовут?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Руку убери!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Настоящее имя!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Пошли ты эту Кристину.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Да ладно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Работу новую найдем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Ты же не животное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Чтоб тебя просто так заставить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Ну да.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Ладно, я с ней поговорю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Лавров, я тебя предупреждал?",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's replay the exchange and look closely at each Russian phrase.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Откуда приехал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Where did you come here from?' The meaning hinges on 'where from' working together with 'came' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Из Воронежа.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'From Voronezh.' Notice how Russian expresses 'out of' and 'Voronezh' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Kristina answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Как зовут?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What is your name?' The grammar packs 'how' and 'call' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Руку убери!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Take your hand off!' Max brings out the contrast between 'hand' and 'take away' in a few short words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Настоящее имя!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Your real name!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'real' before landing on 'name'. Kristina answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Пошли ты эту Кристину.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Tell that Kristina to go to hell.' The plural past ending '-li' on the verb for 'let's go' covers a group or the polite 'you'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Да ладно.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Come on now.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'yes' and finishing on 'really?!'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Работу новую найдем.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'We will find a new job.' Nastya brings out the contrast between 'work' and 'new' in a few short words. Kristina answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Ты же не животное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You are not an animal, after all.' The meaning hinges on 'you' working together with 'animal' in spoken Russian.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Чтоб тебя просто так заставить.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'To be forced into something just like that.' Max anchors the line on 'so that', while 'to force' completes the thought.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Ну да.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, yes.' Nastya anchors the line on 'come on', while 'yes' completes the thought. Kristina answers right away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "kristina",
   "t": "Ладно, я с ней поговорю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'All right, I will go talk to her.' In Russian, the idea of 'really?!' flows naturally into 'I'll talk' without extra filler words.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Лавров, я тебя предупреждал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Lavrov, did I warn you?' Notice how Russian expresses 'Lavrov' and 'warned' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Let's hear the complete conversation once more.",
   "gap": 0.9
